<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mie Prefecture</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mie Prefecture is a prefecture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinai">Kinki region</a> of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ise-bay">Ise Bay</a>, with its capital at Tsu. It was formed in 1876 from the old provinces of Ise, Shima, Iga and part of Kii, after the abolition of the domains in 1871. It contains the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ise-grand-shrine">Ise Grand Shrine</a>, the most sacred shrine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinto">Shinto</a>, and the industrial city of Yokkaichi.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mie-prefecture.jpg" alt="Mie Prefecture" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tawashi2006 · CC BY 2.1 jp · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sunrise_of_the_Wedded_Rocks03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mie forms the eastern part of the Kii Peninsula, about 170 km from north to south and 80 km from east to west, with a coastline of 1,094.9 km. Bordering Aichi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gifu">Gifu</a>, Shiga, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wakayama">Wakayama</a> prefectures, it is counted as part of both the Kansai and Tokai regions, and Kansai dialect is spoken there. It has five geographical areas: the Suzuka Mountains in the north-west, the Ise Plain along the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ise-bay">Ise Bay</a>, where most of the population lives, the Shima Peninsula, the Iga Basin on the border with Nara, and the Nunobiki Mountainous Region in the south. Forest covers about 65 per cent of the land.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Evidence of human habitation in Mie goes back more than 10,000 years, and agricultural communities formed along the rivers and coasts in the Jomon and Yayoi periods. In the seventh century the Saiku Imperial Residence was built in what is now Meiwa to serve as the home and administrative centre of the Saio, an imperial princess who was high priestess of Ise Shrine.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> the area was divided among several feudal domains. Port towns such as Ohminato, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuwana">Kuwana</a> and Anotsu and castle towns flourished along the Tokaido and Ise Roads, and pilgrimages to Ise Shrine became very popular. After the Meiji Restoration the old provinces were reorganised repeatedly: in 1871 the area from the Kiso Three Rivers to Tsu became Anotsu Prefecture and the area south of it Watarai Prefecture. In 1872 the Anotsu seat moved to Yokkaichi and the prefecture was renamed Mie, but the seat returned to Tsu in 1873, and Mie took its present form in 1876, when it merged with Watarai. In 1959 the Ise-wan Typhoon devastated parts of the prefecture and killed many people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Tsu is the capital and Yokkaichi the largest city. The prefecture contains some of the oldest and holiest sites of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinto">Shinto</a>, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ise-grand-shrine">Ise Grand Shrine</a> and the Tsubaki Grand Shrine, and the Kumano Kodo, an old pilgrimage road in the south that is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>. Northern Mie has manufacturing, mainly of vehicles, ships and heavy chemicals, and Honda has run a factory at Suzuka since 1960. The poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsuo-basho">Matsuo Basho</a> was born in Mie, and so were the scholar Norinaga Motoori and the writer Edogawa Ranpo.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mie Prefecture” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
